30:In  contemporary diagnostic  practices,  individuals  presenting  with psychiatric symptom profiles are typically assigned distinct clinical diagnoses. However, accumulating multifactorial evidence suggests that psychiatric  disorders,  including  schizophrenia,  bipolar  disorder  (BD), depression, and attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD), share a common neuropathology (Anon, 2013; Anttila et al., 2018; Barch and Sheffield,  2014;  Patel  et  al.,  2021).  For  instance,  over  50  %  of  individuals meeting criteria for one psychiatric disorder also meet criteria for another (Grisanzio et al., 2018). Moreover, many medications and psychotherapies  have  demonstrated  efficacy  in  reducing  symptoms
42:Psychiatric disorders exhibit a shared neuropathology, yet the diverse presentations among patients necessitate the identification of transdiagnostic subtypes to enhance diagnostic and treatment strategies. This study aims to unveil  potential  transdiagnostic  subtypes  based  on  personalized  gray  matter  morphological  abnormalities.  A total of 496 patients with psychiatric disorders and 255 healthy controls (HCs) from three distinct datasets (one for discovery and two for validation) were enrolled. Individualized gray matter morphological abnormalities were determined using normative modeling to identify transdiagnostic subtypes. In the discovery dataset, two transdiagnostic subtypes with contrasting patterns of structural abnormalities compared to HCs were identified. Reproducibility and generalizability analyses  demonstrated that these  subtypes could be generalized to  new patients and even to new disorders in the validation datasets. These subtypes were characterized by distinct disease epicenters. The gray matter abnormal pattern in subtype 1 was mainly linked to excitatory receptors, whereas subtype 2 showed a predominant association with inhibitory receptors. Furthermore, we observed that the gray matter abnormal pattern in subtype 2 was correlated with transcriptional profiles of inflammationrelated genes, while subtype 1 did not show this association. Our findings reveal two robust transdiagnostic biotypes, offering novel insights into psychiatric nosology.
44:across various psychiatric disorders (Lahey et al., 2012; Lahey et al., 2017).  This  trend  highlights  a  growing  recognition  that  traditional diagnostic  classifications  may  not  fully  capture  the  complexity  and heterogeneity of psychiatric presentations. The identification of transdiagnostic subtypes has emerged as a promising framework to refine our understanding  of  psychiatric  disorders  and  improve  diagnostic  and treatment strategies.
46:Neuroimaging  studies  employing  case-control  designs  and  metaanalyses  consistently  identify  shared  and  disorder-specific  structural abnormalities  in  psychiatric  disorders  (Thompson  et  al.,  2020).  For example, patients with psychiatric disorders commonly exhibit decreased gray matter volume in paralimbic and heteromodal cortical
62:regions (Chang et al., 2018). However, psychiatric disorders are highly heterogeneous syndromes and this variability among cases results in inconsistent or conflicting findings (Chen et al., 2016). Even within a single diagnosis, neuroimaging studies have reported reduced, increased, or even no differential gray matter volumes (L ´ azaro et al., 2011; Okasha et al., 2000). These results suggest the existence of distinct neuroanatomical subtypes in psychiatric disorders. Indeed, studies have identified subtypes with opposing patterns of structural abnormalities within a single diagnosis, such  as schizophrenia and  obsessivecompulsive  disorder  (Chand  et  al.,  2020;  Han  et  al.,  2022b).  To address this heterogeneity, normative modeling has been proposed to characterize structural abnormalities at the individual level by assessing deviations from the normal distribution (Marquand et al., 2016). Using these  individualized  structural  deviations  as  features,  a  recent  study successfully identified two depression biotypes with distinct patterns of cortical thinning and treatment responses (Li et al., 2024). However, whether individualized structural deviations could reveal transdiagnostic subtypes remains unknown.
64:Recent neuroimaging studies suggest that neuropsychiatric disorders target intrinsic brain networks. Pathological perturbations, measured by structural  abnormalities,  typically  originate  in  focal  brain  regions (known  as  'disease  epicenters ' )  and  spread  to  other  brain  regions following  normal  brain  network  architecture,  supporting  a  networkbased  spreading  hypothesis  (Shafiei  et  al.,  2020b;  Wannan  et  al., 2019; Yau et al., 2018; Zhou et al., 2012). A recent study further proposes that regional structural abnormalities could be inferred from topologically  connected  neighbors  (Shafiei  et  al.,  2020a).  Psychiatric disorders and their subtypes are characterized by distinct disease epicenters, reflecting their diverse biological underpinnings and progression trajectories (Hansen et al., 2022; Li et al., 2024). Moreover, with the availability  of  whole-brain  transcriptional  atlas  (Hawrylycz  et  al., 2012a) and neurotransmitter receptors/transporters atlas (Hansen and Shafiei, 2022b), researchers have investigated the transcriptional and molecular substrates underlying structural and functional abnormalities in psychiatric disorders. For instance, the inflammation hypothesis in psychiatric  disorders  posits  that  an  elevated  inflammatory  response induced by factors such as stress exerts direct neurotoxic effects on the brain,  leading  to  structural  and  functional  changes  in  patients  (Berk et al., 2013; Eisenberger et al., 2010; Han and Ham, 2021; Troubat et al., 2021). In this context, a prior study found that transcriptional profiles of inflammation-related  genes  are  associated  with  cortical  thickness decrease in schizophrenia (Cui et al., 2023). Additionally, neurotransmitter dysfunction contributes to cortical abnormalities in psychiatric disorders (Hansen et al., 2022). Investigating the transcriptional and molecular bases  of  structural  abnormalities  helps  bridge  the  gap  between neuroimaging findings and biological mechanisms in psychiatric disorders.
66:In this study, we aimed to identify distinct transdiagnostic subtypes based  on  individualized  gray  matter  morphological  abnormalities compared to normal expectations. We included a total of 496 patients with psychiatric disorders and 255 healthy controls from three independent datasets (one discovery dataset and two validation datasets). First,  we  derived  individualized  deviation  maps  (individualized  gray matter  morphological  abnormalities)  using  normative  modeling  and used  them  to  determine  transdiagnostic  subtypes  in  the  discovery dataset. We then conducted a series of sensitivity analyses to examine the  reproducibility  and  generalizability  of  the  identified  subtypes  in unseen patients and even in unseen disorders in the validation datasets. Subsequently, we investigated the disease epicenters of the identified subtypes. To gain deeper insights into the molecular and genetic underpinnings of the subtypes, we further investigated the associations between structural abnormalities in these subtypes and the distribution of neurotransmitter receptors/transporters, as well as the transcriptional profiles of inflammation-related genes. The workflow of this study is shown in Fig. 1.
72:This study was approved by the research ethics committee of the First Affiliated Hospital of Zhengzhou University (2018-KY-88). A total of 496 patients with psychiatric disorders and 255 heathy controls from three independent datasets (one discovery dataset and two validation datasets) were included in this study. Additionally, another extensive dataset comprising 492 healthy individuals aged 19 -80 was included to construct a normal structural covariance network (see Methods). The discovery dataset was obtained from the University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA) Consortium for Neuropsychiatric Phenomics (CNP) and is available on the public database OpenfMRI (Poldrack et al., 2016).
74:Fig. 1. The workflow of this study. For each brain region, individualized gray matter volume/morphological (GMV) abnormalities are obtained using normative modeling and used to identify transdiagnostic subtypes. A series of sensitivity analyses are conducted to assess the reproducibility and generalizability of clustering outcomes. To gain deeper insights into the subtypes, we investigate their disease epicenters, and molecular and genetic underpinnings.
80:The CNP dataset, approved by the Institutional Review Boards at UCLA and  the  Los  Angeles  County  Department  of  Mental  Health,  includes multimodal  imaging  from  142  patients  with  psychiatric  disorders, including schizophrenia ( n = 50), bipolar disorder (BD, n = 49), and attention  deficit  hyperactivity  disorder  (ADHD, n = 43),  as  well  as healthy controls (HCs, n = 130). Diagnoses were based on the Structured Clinical Interview for DSM-IV-Text Revision (Poldrack et al., 2016). The validation  dataset  1  was  obtained  from  the  Centre  for  Biomedical Research  Excellence  (COBRE)  and  is  available  at  http://fcon\_1000. projects.nitrc.org/indi/retro/cobre.html (Collin et al., 2014). The COBRE group comprised 72 patients with schizophrenia and matched 74 HCs. The validation dataset 2 was obtained from site 20 of the Depression Imaging Research Consortium (DIRECT) (Chen et al., 2022; Yan et al., 2019). The single-site dataset with the largest sample size (site 20) was  used  to  prevent  multi-site  effects  on  the  results.  This  dataset comprised data from 282 patients with depression and 251 matched HCs. All study procedures were in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki of 1975 and approved by the local Research Ethics Committees. More  details  regarding  the  datasets,  data  acquisition,  and  data  preprocessing are described in the Supplementary Methods.
84:First,  we  estimated  individualized  gray  matter  morphological  abnormalities using the normative modeling. Following methods used in previous  studies  (Han  et  al.,  2023a;  Marquand  et  al.,  2016;  Wolfers et al., 2018), we trained a Gaussian process regression model to estimate the normative range of regional gray matter volumes (GMVs) based on age and sex in HCs. This trained model was then applied to each patient, generating predicted GMVs. Subsequently, Z scores (representing individualized abnormalities) were calculated to quantify deviations of the predicted GMVs from normal distributions. Positive Z scores indicated higher GMVs in patients compared to HCs, and vice versa. This process resulted in a Z -score vector (246 × 1) for each patient, representing their individualized gray matter morphological abnormalities.
90:A series of reproducibility and generalizability analyses were conducted to assess the reproducibility and generalizability of the clustering outcomes. Firstly, to ensure that our results were not influenced by a small subset of patients, we randomly selected 90 % of the patients and performed k-means clustering on this subsample, as done previously. We then calculated the Adjusted Rand Index (ARI) between the subtyping results based on this subsample and those based on all patients. This process was repeated 1000 times. Secondly, we validated the subtyping results  using  a  different  clustering  algorithm  called  heterogeneity through  discriminative  analysis  (HYDRA),  which  uses  regional  gray matter volumes (GMVs) as features. HYDRA is a semi-supervised method that identifies distinct subtypes in psychiatric disorders based on differences  between  patients  and  HCs  rather  than  clustering  patients directly  (Varol  et  al.,  2017).  It  adaptively  determines  the  optimal number of subtypes based on the ARI, which quantifies the similarity between  clustering  results  of  cross-validation  (Varol  et  al.,  2017). Thirdly, we assessed whether the identified subtypes could be replicated in independent datasets. We used the cluster centroids of the two subtypes  to  infer  subtype  labels  for  patients  with  schizophrenia  from another independent dataset. Each patient was assigned to one of the two subtypes based on its Euclidean distance to the cluster centroids. We then calculated the ARI between the predicted subtype labels and the true labels obtained using the aforementioned procedures. Furthermore, although  the  subtypes  were  based  on  patients  with  schizophrenia, ADHD, and BD, we investigated whether they could inform subtypes in unseen psychiatric disorders, such as depression. In another dataset of depression,  we  predicted  subtype  labels  for  patients  based  on  their Euclidean distance to the cluster centroids of the two identified subtypes. We then calculated the ARI between the predicted subtype labels and the true labels obtained using the aforementioned procedures.
94:Neuroimaging research has shed light on the impact of psychiatric disorders on brain networks. Gray matter morphological abnormalities, signaling  pathological  progression,  are  bounded  by  the  underlying normal brain network (Shafiei et al., 2020a; Wannan et al., 2019).
96:In our study, we aimed to investigate whether structural abnormalities of the identified subtypes were also influenced by the normal brain network, as assessed through the structural covariance network. This network delineates the coordination of regional volumes among various brain  regions,  potentially  reflecting  shared  developmental  pathways (Alexander-Bloch et al., 2013a; Han et al., 2023c; Yun et al., 2015) and its dysfunction was implicated in the pathology of depression (Kaiser et al., 2015; Lima-Ojeda et al., 2018; Sotiras et al., 2017; Yun et al., 2020). Importantly, previous research has indicated that the structural covariance  network  exhibits  a  stronger  association  with  gray  matter morphological abnormalities in depression compared to the functional network (Han et al., 2023b).
126:The clinical and demographic information of datasets used in this study was included in Table S1.
130:In our discovery dataset, analysis of subtyping results (illustrated in Fig. 2A) revealed the presence of two distinct transdiagnostic subtypes characterized by individualized gray matter morphological abnormalities. Specifically, subtype 1 encompassed 70 patients (schizophrenia, n = 14; BD, n = 35; ADHD, n = 31), while subtype 2 included 44 patients (schizophrenia, n = 23; BD, n = 15; ADHD, n = 6). Disorder composition for each subtype was depicted in Fig. 2B.
136:A series of analyses were conducted to validate the reproducibility and generalizability of our subtyping results. Firstly, the average ARI values (Fig. S2) between subtyping results obtained using all patients and  those  derived  from  randomly  selected  subsets  were  0.96  ( ± 0.25e GLYPH&lt;0&gt; 2 ).  This  finding  mitigated  concerns  regarding  potential  biases introduced by a few patients. Secondly, the optimal number of subtypes as determined by HYDRA remained consistent at 2 when ARI values peaked (Fig. S2). Moreover, the ARI between subtyping results obtained using HYDRA and those reported by us was 0.42. Thirdly, within the validation dataset 1, individualized gray matter morphological abnormalities also revealed two distinct subtypes of schizophrenia exhibiting differential  patterns  relative  to  healthy  controls  (uncorrected  results displayed in Fig. 3, and FDR-corrected results in Fig. S3). Furthermore, these subtypes could be inferred by the cluster centroids obtained from the subtypes identified in the discovery dataset, with an ARI of 0.64 between true subtype labels and inferred ones. These findings confirmed the reproducibility of the identified transdiagnostic subtypes. Fourthly, we investigated whether these identified subtypes could extend to other psychiatric disorders such as depression. Our framework identified two depressive subtypes with contrasting patterns of gray matter morphological  abnormalities  relative  to  HCs  (uncorrected  results  shown  in Fig.  3,  and  FDR-corrected  results  in  Fig.  S3).  The  ARI  between  true subtyping  results  and  those  inferred  by  the  cluster  centroids  of  the identified transdiagnostic subtypes obtained using the discovery dataset was 0.30. These results underscored the generalizability of the identified transdiagnostic subtypes to unseen psychiatric disorders.
172:Using subject-level gray matter volume deviations as features, we successfully identified two robust transdiagnostic subtypes. These subtypes  demonstrated  resilience  across  different  subtyping  algorithms, were replicated  in  independent  datasets,  and  extended  to  previously unseen psychiatric disorders, thereby affirming their reproducibility and generalizability. Notably, they exhibited opposite differential patterns relative  to  healthy  controls  (HCs)  and  distinct  structural  covariance network-informed disease epicenters. Moreover, we observed that gray matter morphological abnormalities in these subtypes were distinctly associated  with  neurotransmitter  receptors/transporters.  Specifically, the abnormal pattern in subtype 1 was predominantly linked to excitatory  receptors,  while  that  in  subtype  2  was  mainly  associated  with inhibitory  receptors,  thereby  providing  insights  into  the  underlying molecular mechanisms of these subtypes. Additionally, we found that the gray matter differential pattern in subtype 2 correlated with transcriptional profiles of inflammation-related genes, whereas such correlation  was  not  evident  in  subtype  1.  This  highlights  the  complex interplay between neurobiological processes and molecular pathways in psychiatric  disorders.  Overall,  our  findings  unveil  two  reproducible transdiagnostic subtypes characterized by distinct gray matter morphological differential patterns and underlying transcriptional and molecular bases. These insights offer valuable contributions to psychiatric nosology, furthering our understanding of the heterogeneous nature of psychiatric disorders.
174:Psychiatric disorders exhibit significant heterogeneity, manifesting in  diverse  symptoms,  disease  trajectories,  treatment  responses,  and neuroimaging profiles among patients (Bondar et al., 2020; Drysdale et  al.,  2017;  Krishnan  and  Nestler,  2008;  Nguyen  et  al.,  2022).  This heterogeneity poses a substantial challenge and often leads to conflicting findings in research (Chen et al., 2016). For instance, Chand et al. identified  two  subtypes  of  schizophrenia:  subtype  1  displayed  widespread decreased gray matter volume across the brain, whereas subtype 2 exhibited increased gray matter volume in the basal ganglia and internal capsule (Chand et al., 2020). Similar subtypes have been reported in  other  psychiatric disorders  as  well  (Han et  al.,  2022b;  Han  et  al., 2023e). In our study, we addressed this heterogeneity by identifying two replicated transdiagnostic subtypes with opposing abnormal gray matter morphological patterns. This reconciliation helps to clarify contradictory findings from previous neuroimaging studies (Bondar et al., 2020; Drysdale et al., 2017; Krishnan and Nestler, 2008; L ´ azaro et al., 2011; Okasha et al., 2000). Although structural abnormalities in subtype 1 were  not  significant  in  the  discovery  dataset,  likely  due  to  its  small sample size, the validation datasets consistently replicated the opposite differential patterns. Furthermore, sensitivity analyses underscored the
182:reproducibility  of  these  identified  subtypes.  Moreover,  despite  being derived from patients with schizophrenia, BD, and ADHD, the cluster centroids of these subtypes successfully generalized to predict subtypes of unseen psychiatric disorders, such as depression. This finding further validates the generalizability of our identified subtypes across various psychiatric conditions.

